            "48x48" : "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9415554eef74e2169ce3e459c177ff75?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FLP-4.png"
         },
         "displayName" : "Lewis Pringle",
         "self" : "https://stroika.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3A4fe560ca-b4e1-41d1-9bdf-97b1684eda32",
         "timeZone" : "America/New_York"
      },
      "customfield_10012" : "0|i004hz:",
      "customfield_10300" : "{}",
      "description" : {
         "content" : [
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "I've long intended to do an implementation Set_BitString<> (or Sequence_BItString).",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            },
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "But talking to Tom (Tom Pringle) about a problem he's working on, I realized this could be pretty easily used to store arbitrary (fixed) sized objects.",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            },
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "So for example, ",
                     "type" : "text"
                  },
                  {
                     "type" : "hardBreak"
                  },
                  {
                     "text" : "template <typename T, size_t nBits, typename BaseType=std:::byte>",
                     "type" : "text"
                  },
                  {
                     "type" : "hardBreak"
                  },
                  {
                     "text" : "class Sequence_PackedBits : Sequence<BaseType> {",
                     "type" : "text"
                  },
                  {
                     "type" : "hardBreak"
                  },
                  {
                     "text" : "};",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            },
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "// like vector, except packs at the bit level, so Sequence_PackedBits <T, 4> would act like a regular Sequence<T>, but would use 1/2 the space of Sequence_vector<>;",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            },
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "I guess this doesn't make sense for Set ... there we really just would use Set_BitString<> - because the index is the number stored in the set, and it only makes sense to use 1 bit there cuz the only relevant property is contains or not.",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            },
            {
               "content" : [
                  {
                     "text" : "You COULD use this PackedBits trick for MultiSet<T> however.  MultiSet_PackedBits, could be implemented as an array of nbits per element of how many are in the multiset.",
                     "type" : "text"
                  }
               ],
               "type" : "paragraph"
            }
         ],
         "type" : "doc",
         "version" : 1
      },
